## How to use

Everything in a Janus game is an **entity** (an ID) with **components** (plain structs of data). **Systems** are functions that run every frame for every entity that has a given set of components. The engine runs them in Engine::Run().

<b>1. Get the world.</b> There is one flecs world, created on first use. ECS::GetWorld() returns it; use the normal [flecs C++ API](https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/md_docs_2EntitiesComponents.html) on it.

```cpp
flecs::world& world = ECS::GetWorld();
```

<b>2. Define components</b> with #DEFINE_COMPONENT, inside `namespace Components`. Each member is an #ECS_MEMBER with a type, a name and a default value:

\genexopen{ecs/Movement.cpp,Three components}
\snippet ecs/Movement.cpp components
\endex

<b>3. Register them</b> after Engine::InitializePlugins() with #ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT. This adds flecs reflection data (so the components can be printed, saved and edited) and a panel in the Entity Debugger:

```cpp
ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT(FVelocity, world);
```

<b>4. Create entities</b> and give them components with `set` (with a value) or `add` (default value):

\genexopen{ecs/Movement.cpp,An entity with engine and game components}
\snippet ecs/Movement.cpp player
\endex

<b>5. Add systems.</b> A system lists the components it needs; its function runs once per matching entity, every frame:

\genexopen{ecs/Movement.cpp,Three systems}
\snippet ecs/Movement.cpp systems
\endex

<b>6. Inspect it live.</b> Open *Entity Debugger → Entity Outliner* in the running game to see entities with an `FTransformComponent`, select one, and edit its registered components.

## Built-in components

| Component | Members (default) | What uses it | Defined |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Components::FTransformComponent | `glm::vec3 Location (0,0,0)`, `glm::vec3 Rotation (0,0,0)`, `glm::vec3 Scale (0,0,0)` | Renderer (position; `Rotation.x` in radians), camera, lights, Entity Debugger, mouse hover | \src{include/Gameplay/ECS/Modules/TransformModule.hpp,16,20} |
| Components::FBoundingBoxComponent | `glm::vec4 Bounds (0,32,0,32)`, `float Height 32`, `float Width 32`, `glm::vec2 Offset (0,0)` | Mouse hover (`Width`, `Height`, `Offset`) | \src{include/Gameplay/ECS/Modules/TransformModule.hpp,28,33} |
| Components::FCameraComponent | `glm::vec2 CameraSize (30,15)`, `glm::vec2 CenterSize (32,32)`, `glm::vec4 Boundaries (0,2000,0,2000)` | Renderer: the view | \src{include/Gameplay/ECS/Modules/GraphicsModule.hpp,23,27} |
| Components::FLightComponent | `float Radius 0.15`, `float Falloff -0.3`, `float Intensity 1`, `glm::vec2 PositionOffset (0,0)`, `SDL_FColor RGB (1,1,1,1)` | Renderer: lighting | \src{include/Gameplay/ECS/Modules/GraphicsModule.hpp,15,21} |
| Components::FTexture2DComponent | Sprite, size, colour | Renderer: sprites | \ref rendering |
| Components::FRenderingComponent | Render layer | Renderer: sprites | \ref rendering |
| Components::FColliderComponent, Components::FMouseState | Collider shape; mouse position and hovered entity | GameplayFramework | \ref gameplay |

The camera and light are explained in \ref rendering. The engine registers reflection data for `glm::vec2`, `glm::vec3`, `glm::vec4`, `SDL_FColor`, `std::string` and Filesystem::SSoftAssetPath, so components can use those as member types (\src{src/Gameplay/ECS/Module.cpp,39,55}).

## Defining components {#ecs_components}

| Macro | Makes | Register with |
| --- | --- | --- |
| #DEFINE_COMPONENT `(Name, members...)` | The struct, reflection data and an Entity Debugger panel | #ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT |
| #DEFINE_COMPONENT_NO_DEBUG `(Name, members...)` | The struct and reflection data, no panel | #ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT_NO_DEBUG |
| #DEFINE_COMPONENT_OVERRIDE_DEBUG `(Name, Pre, Post, members...)` | Like #DEFINE_COMPONENT, but calls your `Pre(Name*)` and `Post(Name*)` around the automatic widgets | #ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT |

\genexopen{ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp,All three variants\, and plain flecs tags}
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp components
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp debug_draw
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp register
\endex

Rules:

- Use the macros **inside `namespace Components`**; the registration macros refer to `Components::Name`.
- A component with no members (`DEFINE_COMPONENT(FPlayer)`) works as a marker. Plain empty structs also work as flecs *tags*, without registration, but don't appear in the Entity Debugger.
- The Entity Debugger draws members of type `float`, `glm::vec2/3/4`, `SDL_FColor`, Filesystem::SSoftAssetPath and Renderer::EInstanceLayer (\src{include/Gameplay/ECS/Components.hpp,125,170}). Members of other types (`int`, `bool`, ...) are stored and reflected but not shown. To draw another type, specialize `Components::ECSDebug_DrawElement<T>` the way \src{include/Rendering/SpriteInstanceContainer.hpp,28,34} does for render layers.
- Member types must be known to flecs reflection: built-in types, or one of the types listed above.

## Systems, phases and queries

flecs runs systems in **phases**, in order: `OnLoad`, `PostLoad`, `PreUpdate`, `OnUpdate` (the default), `OnValidate`, `PostUpdate`, `PreStore`, `OnStore`. Choose one with `.kind(...)`. The engine adds:

| Phase | Runs after | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ECS::Phase::OnInput()` | `PostUpdate` | Defined by the Input module (\src{include/Input/Module.hpp,9}); ProtoRPG moves its player here |
| `ECS::Phase::OnCollision` | `PostUpdate` | Defined at startup for collision checks (\src{src/Gameplay/ECS/Module.cpp,58}) |
| `ECS::Phase::OnPreRender()` | `OnStore` | The renderer gathers sprites, lights and the camera here (\src{include/Rendering/Module.hpp,18}) |

Add your own with #DEFINE_NEW_ECS_PHASE:

\genexopen{ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp,A custom phase and systems that use it}
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp phase
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp systems
\endex

<b>Queries</b> find entities outside a system's own match, for collision checks or counting. Build them once and keep them:

\genexopen{ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp,Queries filtered by tags}
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp queries
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp build_queries
\endex

<b>Observers</b> run as soon as something happens to an entity (a component added, set or removed) instead of every frame:

\genexopen{ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp,Observers}
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp observers
\endex

<b>Prefabs</b> hold components that many entities start with:

\genexopen{ecs/Spawning.cpp,A prefab and its instances}
\snippet ecs/Spawning.cpp prefab
\snippet ecs/Spawning.cpp spawn
\endex

The flecs manual covers all of these in depth: [systems](https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/md_docs_2Systems.html), [queries](https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/md_docs_2Queries.html), [observers](https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/md_docs_2ObserversManual.html), [prefabs](https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/md_docs_2PrefabsManual.html).

## The global entity and selection

- ECS::GetGlobalSystemsEntity() returns one entity created on first use. Store game-wide settings on it as components; the Entity Debugger shows them under **Global Entity**. The mouse-hover system keeps its Components::FMouseState there.
- ECS::SelectEntity() chooses which entity the Entity Debugger shows; ECS::GetSelectedEntity() returns it.

\genexopen{ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp,Settings on the global entity}
\snippet ecs/ComponentsAndQueries.cpp global_entity
\endex

## Things to watch out for

- Changes made inside a system (creating, destroying, `set`, `add`, `remove`) are **deferred** until the system finishes. Reading a component you just `set` on another entity in the same system returns the old value, and an entity created in the system has no components yet (use `try_get`).
- Outside systems (for example in an editor tool's ImGui code), changes are immediate. Wrap loops that destroy entities in `world.defer(...)`.
- ECS::Module() gives flecs 8 worker threads (\src{src/Gameplay/ECS/Module.cpp,34}), but systems only use them if you add `.multi_threaded()`. Engine and example systems are single-threaded.
- The Entity Outliner only lists entities that have an `FTransformComponent`.
- The Entity Debugger's **Save Entity** button writes a flecs script to `Resources/Output/ECS/<name>`, which must already exist. Load such a file with `world.script_run(name, text)`.
- ECS::TeardownModule() destroys the world, which then is destroyed again at exit; see \ref known_issues_shutdown.
*/

